"""In-depth material for the Algorithms lessons."""
from learn import deepen

deepen("algo-asymptotics",
       proofs=[
           ("f(n) = Θ(g(n)) ⇔ f = O(g) and f = Ω(g); the limit test",
            """By definition f = O(g) means ∃c, n₀: f(n) ≤ c·g(n) for n ≥ n₀, and f = Ω(g) means ∃c′, n₀′: f(n) ≥ c′·g(n) for n ≥ n₀′. Taking max(n₀, n₀′) both inequalities hold together, which is exactly c′·g ≤ f ≤ c·g, i.e. Θ. **Limit test:** if L = lim f(n)/g(n) exists then L = 0 ⇒ f = o(g); 0 < L < ∞ ⇒ f = Θ(g); L = ∞ ⇒ f = ω(g). For 0 < L < ∞, for large n the ratio lies in (L/2, 2L), giving the two constants. Example: (n² + 3n)/n² → 1, so n² + 3n = Θ(n²)."""),
           ("The ladder of growth rates: log n ≪ nᵉ ≪ cⁿ ≪ n!",
            """For any ε > 0, ln n / nᵉ → 0: substitute n = eᵗ to get t/e^(εt) → 0 (the exponential beats the polynomial, e.g. by L'Hôpital). For any polynomial and c > 1, nᵏ/cⁿ → 0: apply the previous fact to cⁿ = e^(n ln c) repeatedly or L'Hôpital k times. For factorials, n! ≥ (n/2)^(n/2), which dominates every cⁿ, and Stirling's formula n! ≈ √(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ shows log(n!) = Θ(n log n). Therefore: constants < log log n < log n < √n < n < n log n < n² < n³ < 2ⁿ < n! < nⁿ."""),
           ("Counting loop iterations: Σ and harmonic sums",
            """A loop `for i = 1 to n: for j = 1 to i: work` executes Σ_{i=1}^{n} i = n(n + 1)/2 = Θ(n²) times. `for (i = 1; i < n; i *= 2)` runs ⌈log₂ n⌉ times because the exponent doubles. `for i = 1 to n: for (j = i; j ≤ n; j += i)` performs Σ ⌊n/i⌋ ≈ n·Hₙ = Θ(n log n) steps since the harmonic number Hₙ = 1 + ½ + … + 1/n ≈ ln n (compare the sum with ∫dx/x). A loop whose variable is replaced by its square root each time, `n → √n`, executes log log n times: after k steps n^(1/2ᵏ) ≤ 2 when 2ᵏ ≥ log₂ n."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Rules for manipulating asymptotic expressions",
            """O(f) + O(g) = O(max(f, g)); O(f)·O(g) = O(fg); constants and lower-order terms are dropped; logarithm bases differ by a constant (log_a n = Θ(log_b n)), but exponent bases do not: 2ⁿ ≠ Θ(3ⁿ), and n^(log n) is super-polynomial. Little-o and little-ω are strict versions. Transitivity holds for all five relations, symmetry only for Θ, and f = O(g) ⇔ g = Ω(f). A function and its square root, or n and n + 1 inside a polynomial, give the same Θ class; however 2^(n+1) = Θ(2ⁿ) while 2^(2n) = 4ⁿ is not Θ(2ⁿ)."""),
           ("Best, worst and average case; amortised analysis",
            """The **worst case** bounds the running time over all inputs of size n; the **average case** averages over a distribution of inputs; **amortised** analysis bounds the average cost per operation over a sequence of operations even when single operations are expensive. Example: a dynamic array that doubles when full performs n appends in total O(n) work (copy costs 1 + 2 + 4 + … + n < 2n), so each append is O(1) amortised, although a single append can cost Θ(n). The accounting argument: charge 3 units per append, 1 for the insertion and 2 stored as credit to pay for copying later."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-recurrences-dc",
       proofs=[
           ("Master theorem for T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n), by the recursion tree",
            """Level i of the recursion tree has aⁱ nodes, each of size n/bⁱ and cost f(n/bⁱ), so the level cost is aⁱ f(n/bⁱ). The tree has depth log_b n and there are a^(log_b n) = n^(log_b a) leaves, each costing Θ(1), so the leaves contribute Θ(n^(log_b a)). The total is the sum of the level costs. Three cases:

1. **f(n) = O(n^(log_b a − ε))**: the level costs grow geometrically toward the leaves, so the leaf level dominates: T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a)).
2. **f(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))**: every level costs about the same, Θ(n^(log_b a)), and there are log_b n levels: T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a) log n).
3. **f(n) = Ω(n^(log_b a + ε)) and a f(n/b) ≤ c f(n) for some c < 1** (regularity): the level costs decrease geometrically from the root, so the root dominates: T(n) = Θ(f(n)).

Example: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n: n^(log₂ 2) = n matches f(n), case 2 gives Θ(n log n). T(n) = 4T(n/2) + n: n² beats n, case 1 gives Θ(n²)."""),
           ("Solving T(n) = T(n − 1) + n and T(n) = 2T(n − 1) + 1 by unrolling",
            """T(n) = T(n − 1) + n = T(n − 2) + (n − 1) + n = … = T(0) + 1 + 2 + … + n = T(0) + n(n + 1)/2 = Θ(n²). For T(n) = 2T(n − 1) + 1 with T(0) = 0: T(n) = 2(2T(n − 2) + 1) + 1 = 4T(n − 2) + 2 + 1 = … = 2ⁿT(0) + (2ⁿ⁻¹ + … + 2 + 1) = 2ⁿ − 1. This is the number of moves in the Tower of Hanoi. For T(n) = T(n/2) + 1: after k halvings n/2ᵏ = 1 so T(n) = Θ(log n) (binary search); for T(n) = T(n/2) + n the sum n + n/2 + n/4 + … = 2n gives Θ(n)."""),
           ("Why merge sort is Θ(n log n) and why comparison sorts need Ω(n log n)",
            """Merge sort satisfies T(n) = 2T(n/2) + cn: there are log₂ n levels each costing cn. **Lower bound:** any comparison sort is a binary decision tree whose leaves are the possible orderings of the input; there must be at least n! leaves because each permutation needs its own output. A binary tree with L leaves has height at least log₂ L, so the worst-case number of comparisons is at least log₂(n!) = Θ(n log n) (since n! ≥ (n/2)^(n/2) gives log₂ n! ≥ (n/2) log₂(n/2)). Hence merge sort and heap sort are asymptotically optimal among comparison sorts."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("When the master theorem does not apply",
            """It requires T(n) = aT(n/b) + f(n) with constants a ≥ 1, b > 1 and f polynomially comparable to n^(log_b a). It fails for T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n/log n (f is only logarithmically smaller; the answer is Θ(n log log n) by the recursion tree), for T(n) = T(n/3) + T(2n/3) + n (use the recursion tree: Θ(n log n), every level costs ≤ n and the depth is log_{3/2} n), for a that depends on n, and for subtraction recurrences T(n) = aT(n − b) + f(n), which are exponential when a > 1. Substitution (guess and verify by induction) is the general fallback: guess T(n) ≤ cn log n, assume it for smaller sizes and check the inequality."""),
           ("Divide-and-conquer examples to remember",
            """Binary search T(n) = T(n/2) + 1 = Θ(log n); merge sort and the maximum-subarray problem Θ(n log n); Karatsuba multiplication T(n) = 3T(n/2) + n = Θ(n^log₂3) ≈ n^1.585; Strassen matrix multiplication T(n) = 7T(n/2) + n² = Θ(n^log₂7) ≈ n^2.81; quickselect average Θ(n); finding both the minimum and the maximum needs ⌈3n/2⌉ − 2 comparisons; closest pair of points Θ(n log n). Quick sort's recurrence is T(n) = T(k) + T(n − k − 1) + n, worst case Θ(n²) when the split is always 0 : n − 1."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-sorting-searching",
       proofs=[
           ("Quick sort average case is Θ(n log n)",
            """Let C(n) be the expected number of comparisons with a uniformly random pivot. The pivot splits the array into sizes k and n − 1 − k with k uniform in {0, …, n − 1}: C(n) = (n − 1) + (1/n)Σ_{k=0}^{n−1}[C(k) + C(n − 1 − k)] = (n − 1) + (2/n)Σ C(k). Multiply by n: nC(n) = n(n − 1) + 2ΣC(k); subtract the same relation for n − 1: nC(n) − (n − 1)C(n − 1) = 2(n − 1) + 2C(n − 1), so nC(n) = (n + 1)C(n − 1) + 2(n − 1). Dividing by n(n + 1): C(n)/(n + 1) = C(n − 1)/n + 2(n − 1)/(n(n + 1)) ≈ C(n − 1)/n + 2/n. Telescoping gives C(n)/(n + 1) ≈ 2Hₙ, hence **C(n) ≈ 2n ln n ≈ 1.39 n log₂ n**, only 39% more than the optimum."""),
           ("Heap construction is O(n)",
            """Heapify sifts down every internal node starting from the last. A node at height h costs at most h swaps and there are at most ⌈n/2ʰ⁺¹⌉ nodes of height h. Total work = Σ_{h=0}^{log n} ⌈n/2ʰ⁺¹⌉·h ≤ n Σ h/2ʰ = n·2 = O(n), using Σ_{h≥0} h/2ʰ = 2. So building a heap is linear, while n successive insertions cost Θ(n log n). Heap sort then performs n − 1 extract-max operations at O(log n) each: Θ(n log n) in all cases, in place, but not stable."""),
           ("Stability and the number of inversions",
            """A sort is **stable** if equal keys keep their relative order. Insertion sort moves an element left only past strictly larger elements, hence stable; selection sort swaps non-adjacent elements and is not stable in general; merge sort is stable if ties take the left element first. **Inversions:** pairs i < j with a[i] > a[j]. Each adjacent swap of bubble/insertion sort removes exactly one inversion, so these sorts perform exactly (number of inversions) swaps. A reversed array has n(n − 1)/2 inversions and a random permutation has n(n − 1)/4 on average, because every pair is inverted with probability ½. Merge sort counts inversions in Θ(n log n) by adding the number of remaining left-run elements whenever a right element is taken."""),
           ("Counting sort and radix sort",
            """Counting sort for keys in 0..k counts occurrences (O(n + k)), takes prefix sums to obtain each key's final positions and places the elements from right to left to stay stable: Θ(n + k) time and space, no comparisons so the n log n bound does not apply. Radix sort sorts d-digit numbers by applying a stable sort to each digit from least significant to most: Θ(d(n + k)). Correctness: after processing digit i the numbers are sorted by their last i digits, because ties on digit i are resolved by the previous order (stability)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Comparison table",
            """Bubble/insertion/selection: Θ(n²) worst, with insertion Θ(n) on sorted input and bubble Θ(n) with an early-exit flag; selection always makes exactly n(n − 1)/2 comparisons but only n − 1 swaps. Merge Θ(n log n) always, O(n) extra space. Quick: Θ(n log n) average, Θ(n²) worst (sorted input with a first/last pivot), in place, not stable. Heap: Θ(n log n), in place. Insertion sort is adaptive and best for small or nearly sorted input; binary insertion reduces comparisons to O(n log n) but not moves. Minimum number of comparisons to find the maximum is n − 1; the median can be found in O(n) (median of medians)."""),
           ("Searching",
            """Linear search averages (n + 1)/2 comparisons for a successful search; binary search needs at most ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1 comparisons on a sorted array. Interpolation search averages O(log log n) for uniform data. Searching in a sorted matrix (rows and columns sorted) from a corner takes O(m + n). Hash tables give O(1) expected search. The number of different outcomes of a binary search with n elements is 2n + 1 (n hits and n + 1 gaps), which gives the log bound by the same decision-tree argument as sorting."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-greedy",
       proofs=[
           ("Greedy-choice for activity selection: choosing the earliest finishing activity is safe",
            """Let S be the activities sorted by finish time and let A* be any optimal solution. Let a₁ be the activity that finishes first. If a₁ ∈ A* we are done. Otherwise let b be the first activity of A* (by finish time). Since a₁ finishes no later than b, replacing b by a₁ keeps A* feasible (a₁ does not overlap any later activity of A*, because those start after b finishes, which is at or after a₁ finishes). The result is still optimal and contains a₁. After selecting a₁ the remaining problem is the same problem on the activities starting after a₁ finishes, so by induction the greedy algorithm builds an optimal set. Running time Θ(n log n) for the sort and O(n) for the scan."""),
           ("Huffman coding is optimal (exchange argument)",
            """Let x and y be the two least frequent symbols. **Claim:** there is an optimal prefix code in which x and y are sibling leaves at the maximum depth. Take an optimal tree and two deepest sibling leaves a and b. Swapping x with a: the cost changes by (f(a) − f(x))(depth(x) − depth(a)) ≤ 0 since f(x) ≤ f(a) and depth(a) ≥ depth(x). Similarly swap y with b. After the swaps x and y are deepest siblings and the cost has not increased. Merging x and y into one symbol of weight f(x) + f(y) reduces the cost by exactly f(x) + f(y); an optimal tree for the smaller alphabet therefore extends to an optimal tree for the original. Induction on the alphabet size finishes the proof. The cost is Σ f(c)·depth(c); the algorithm runs in O(n log n) with a heap."""),
           ("Fractional knapsack is solved greedily, 0/1 knapsack is not",
            """Sort items by value per unit weight and take them whole in that order, then a fraction of the next one. Exchange argument: suppose an optimal solution takes less of an item i with higher ratio than it takes of an item j with lower ratio; swap a small amount δ of weight from j to i: the value changes by δ(vᵢ/wᵢ − vⱼ/wⱼ) > 0, a contradiction. For 0/1 knapsack, capacity 50 with items (w, v) = (10, 60), (20, 100), (30, 120) shows the failure: greedy by ratio picks 10 and 20 for 160, while 20 and 30 give 220. That problem needs dynamic programming."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Recognising greedy problems",
            """Greedy works when the problem has the **greedy-choice property** (a locally optimal choice is part of some global optimum) and **optimal substructure**. Typical GATE greedy problems: activity selection, Huffman coding, fractional knapsack, job sequencing with deadlines (sort by profit, place each job in the latest free slot before its deadline), minimum spanning trees (Kruskal, Prim), Dijkstra with non-negative weights, coin change with canonical coin systems (the greedy fails for {1, 3, 4} and amount 6: 4 + 1 + 1 versus 3 + 3). Always try a small counter-example before trusting a greedy rule."""),
           ("Huffman bookkeeping",
            """For n symbols the tree has n leaves and n − 1 internal nodes, and the merge step is performed n − 1 times. The total cost of an optimal code equals the sum of the weights of all internal nodes. Codewords form a prefix code, so decoding is unambiguous and online. The average code length lies within one bit of the entropy H = −Σpᵢ log₂ pᵢ. If all frequencies are equal powers of two Huffman reproduces the fixed-length structure; with n = 2ᵏ equal frequencies every code has length k."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-dynamic-programming",
       proofs=[
           ("LCS: optimal substructure and the recurrence",
            """Let X = x₁…xₘ, Y = y₁…yₙ and let Z = z₁…z_k be an LCS. **If xₘ = yₙ**, then z_k = xₘ = yₙ (otherwise append it and get a longer common subsequence) and Z with its last symbol removed is an LCS of X₁..ₘ₋₁ and Y₁..ₙ₋₁. **If xₘ ≠ yₙ** then z_k differs from at least one of them; if z_k ≠ xₘ, Z is a common subsequence of X₁..ₘ₋₁ and Y, so the LCS is the better of the two ways of dropping a last symbol. Hence L[i][j] = L[i − 1][j − 1] + 1 if xᵢ = yⱼ, else max(L[i − 1][j], L[i][j − 1]), with L = 0 on the border. The table has (m + 1)(n + 1) cells filled in constant time each: Θ(mn) time, and O(min(m, n)) space if only the LCS length is needed."""),
           ("0/1 knapsack recurrence and pseudo-polynomial time",
            """Let K[i][w] be the best value using the first i items with capacity w. Item i is either skipped, giving K[i − 1][w], or taken (if wᵢ ≤ w), giving vᵢ + K[i − 1][w − wᵢ]. Hence K[i][w] = max of those two, with K[0][·] = 0. Filling an n × W table takes Θ(nW) time. Because W is a number whose size in bits is log W, nW is exponential in the input length: the algorithm is **pseudo-polynomial**, consistent with the problem being NP-complete."""),
           ("Matrix-chain multiplication",
            """For matrices Aᵢ of size p_{i−1} × pᵢ, let m[i][j] be the minimum number of scalar multiplications for Aᵢ…Aⱼ. The last multiplication splits the chain at some k: (Aᵢ…Aₖ)(Aₖ₊₁…Aⱼ), costing m[i][k] + m[k + 1][j] + p_{i−1}pₖpⱼ. Hence m[i][j] = min over i ≤ k < j of that expression, with m[i][i] = 0. There are Θ(n²) subproblems and each takes O(n) to evaluate: Θ(n³) time, Θ(n²) space. For dimensions 10×100, 100×5, 5×50: (A₁A₂)A₃ costs 10·100·5 + 10·5·50 = 7500 versus A₁(A₂A₃) = 100·5·50 + 10·100·50 = 75,000."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("How to design a DP",
            """1) Define the state precisely (what does table[i][j] mean?). 2) Write the recurrence using the last decision. 3) Fix the base cases. 4) Choose an order of evaluation so that dependencies are filled first (increasing length for interval DP; row by row for LCS). 5) Read the answer and, if required, backtrack the decisions. The number of subproblems times the cost per subproblem gives the running time. Memoisation (top-down) computes only needed states; tabulation (bottom-up) avoids recursion overhead. DP applies when subproblems **overlap** — if they do not (merge sort) it is just divide and conquer."""),
           ("Other classic DPs",
            """Edit distance: D[i][j] = min(D[i − 1][j] + 1, D[i][j − 1] + 1, D[i − 1][j − 1] + [xᵢ ≠ yⱼ]), Θ(mn). Longest increasing subsequence: Θ(n²) by DP or Θ(n log n) with patience sorting. Coin change (number of ways): ways[a] = Σ over coins. Rod cutting: r[n] = max(p[i] + r[n − i]). Floyd–Warshall: d_k[i][j] = min(d_{k−1}[i][j], d_{k−1}[i][k] + d_{k−1}[k][j]), Θ(n³). Fibonacci with memoisation: O(n) instead of exponential, since the call tree without memoisation has Fₙ leaves ≈ φⁿ."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-graph-traversal",
       proofs=[
           ("BFS computes shortest path distances in an unweighted graph",
            """Claim: when BFS dequeues a vertex v, its recorded distance d(v) equals the true shortest-path distance δ(s, v). Proof by induction on δ. Vertices at distance 0 (just s) are correct. Suppose all vertices at distance < k are correct and v has δ(s, v) = k; it has a neighbour u with δ(s, u) = k − 1. BFS processes vertices in non-decreasing order of d (the queue holds vertices with distances differing by at most 1 in a sorted pattern), so u is dequeued before any vertex at distance ≥ k and, when scanning u's neighbours, v is discovered with d(v) = d(u) + 1 = k unless it was discovered earlier, which could only be from a vertex with smaller d — impossible since then δ(s, v) < k. Each vertex and edge is examined once: Θ(V + E) with adjacency lists, Θ(V²) with a matrix."""),
           ("Properties of DFS: edge classification and the white-path theorem",
            """DFS records discovery d[v] and finish f[v] times. **White-path theorem:** v is a descendant of u in the DFS forest iff at time d[u] there is a path from u to v consisting solely of undiscovered (white) vertices. Hence intervals [d, f] are either nested or disjoint (parenthesis theorem). For an edge (u, v): tree edge if v is white; back edge if v is a gray ancestor (it exists ⇔ the graph has a cycle); forward edge if v is a black descendant; cross edge otherwise. **A directed graph is acyclic iff DFS finds no back edge**: a back edge closes a cycle with the tree path; conversely, a cycle's first discovered vertex u has a white path to the vertex v before it on the cycle, so v becomes a descendant of u and the edge (v, u) is a back edge."""),
           ("Topological sort by decreasing finish time, and Kahn's algorithm",
            """In a DAG, for every edge (u, v) we have f[u] > f[v]: if DFS reaches u first, v is white or black — it cannot be gray (that would be a back edge), so v finishes before u; if it reaches v first, v finishes before u can even be discovered since no path from v leads back to u. Hence listing vertices by decreasing finish time is a topological order. Kahn's algorithm repeatedly removes a vertex of indegree 0: a DAG always has one (otherwise follow incoming edges backward forever and a cycle appears), and if the algorithm stalls with vertices left, those vertices all lie on or behind cycles. Both run in Θ(V + E). The number of topological orderings can be exponential (an empty graph on n vertices has n!)."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Strongly connected components and articulation points",
            """Kosaraju: run DFS on G to get finish times, then DFS on the transpose Gᵀ in decreasing order of finish time; each DFS tree of the second pass is one strongly connected component. Tarjan computes them in one pass using low-link values. The component graph is a DAG. In an undirected graph, vertex v is an articulation point if removing it disconnects the graph: the DFS root is one iff it has ≥ 2 children; another vertex v is one iff some child w has low[w] ≥ d[v]. A bridge is a tree edge (v, w) with low[w] > d[v]."""),
           ("Counting with BFS and DFS",
            """The number of connected components can be counted by starting a traversal from each unvisited vertex. A graph is bipartite iff BFS can 2-colour it (no edge joins two vertices at the same level parity). In a BFS tree of an undirected graph every non-tree edge joins vertices whose levels differ by at most 1; in a DFS tree every non-tree edge joins an ancestor with a descendant (no cross edges). Space: BFS can hold Θ(V) vertices in the queue; DFS uses the stack depth up to V. With an adjacency matrix both need Θ(V²) time."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-shortest-paths-mst",
       proofs=[
           ("Correctness of Dijkstra's algorithm (non-negative weights)",
            """Invariant: when a vertex u is extracted from the priority queue, d[u] = δ(s, u). Suppose u is the first vertex extracted with d[u] > δ(s, u). Consider a shortest path from s to u and let y be the first vertex on it that is not yet extracted, with x its predecessor (already extracted, so d[x] = δ(s, x) and the edge (x, y) was relaxed): d[y] = δ(s, y). Because weights are non-negative, δ(s, y) ≤ δ(s, u) ≤ d[u]. But u was extracted before y, so d[u] ≤ d[y] = δ(s, y) ≤ δ(s, u), giving d[u] = δ(s, u), a contradiction. Negative edges break the step δ(s, y) ≤ δ(s, u). With a binary heap the running time is O((V + E) log V), with a Fibonacci heap O(E + V log V), with an array O(V²)."""),
           ("The cut property and correctness of Prim and Kruskal",
            """**Cut property.** For any cut (S, V ∖ S) of the graph, a minimum-weight edge e crossing the cut belongs to some minimum spanning tree. Proof: take an MST T without e; adding e to T creates a cycle, which crosses the cut at another edge f; removing f gives a spanning tree T′ = T + e − f with weight w(T) + w(e) − w(f) ≤ w(T) because w(e) ≤ w(f). So T′ is also an MST containing e. Prim grows one tree and always adds the lightest edge crossing the cut (tree, rest) — safe by the cut property. Kruskal adds the globally lightest edge joining two components — safe by the cut property applied to the cut separating one of those components. With distinct weights the MST is unique. The **cycle property**: the heaviest edge on any cycle (unique max) is in no MST."""),
           ("Bellman–Ford and Floyd–Warshall",
            """After i rounds of relaxing every edge, d[v] is at most the weight of the shortest path using at most i edges (induction: a path with i edges extends a path with i − 1 edges by one relaxed edge). A shortest simple path has at most V − 1 edges, so V − 1 rounds suffice, and a V-th round that still improves some distance proves a reachable negative cycle. Time Θ(VE). Floyd–Warshall computes all pairs with dᵏ[i][j] = min(dᵏ⁻¹[i][j], dᵏ⁻¹[i][k] + dᵏ⁻¹[k][j]) over intermediate vertices {1..k}: Θ(V³) time, Θ(V²) space; a negative diagonal entry reveals a negative cycle."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("Which algorithm for which problem",
            """Unweighted single source: BFS Θ(V + E). Non-negative weights: Dijkstra. Negative edges, no negative cycles: Bellman–Ford. DAG: relax edges in topological order, Θ(V + E) (also gives longest paths by negating weights). All pairs: Floyd–Warshall or V × Dijkstra. MST: Kruskal O(E log E) (sort + union–find, near-linear after sorting), Prim O(E log V) with a heap. A graph with V vertices has an MST with V − 1 edges; the number of distinct MST edges weights is the same across MSTs (the sorted weight multiset is identical); if all edge weights are distinct the MST is unique; adding a constant to every weight does not change the MST but can change the shortest paths."""),
           ("Union–find",
            """Disjoint-set forests with **union by rank** and **path compression** support m operations on n elements in O(m α(n)) where α is the inverse Ackermann function (≤ 4 for any practical n). Without compression but with union by rank each operation costs O(log n). Kruskal uses Find to test whether an edge joins two different components and Union to merge them."""),
       ])

deepen("algo-complexity-classes",
       proofs=[
           ("P ⊆ NP, and polynomial-time reductions compose",
            """If a language L is decided in polynomial time by a deterministic machine M, then M is also a (trivial) verifier that ignores any certificate, so L ∈ NP. For reductions: suppose A ≤ₚ B via f (computable in time p(n)) and B ≤ₚ C via g (time q(n)). Then g∘f maps instances of A to instances of C, preserves yes-instances (x ∈ A ⇔ f(x) ∈ B ⇔ g(f(x)) ∈ C), and runs in time p(n) + q(p(n)), a polynomial. Hence ≤ₚ is transitive, which is why NP-completeness can be propagated along a chain of reductions."""),
           ("If a polynomial-time algorithm solves any NP-complete problem then P = NP",
            """Let L be NP-complete with L ∈ P. For any problem A ∈ NP, by completeness A ≤ₚ L: map an instance of A to an instance of L in polynomial time and run the polynomial algorithm for L. The composition is a polynomial algorithm for A, so NP ⊆ P, and together with P ⊆ NP we get P = NP. Conversely if P ≠ NP no NP-complete problem has a polynomial algorithm. This is why proving one problem NP-complete (Cook–Levin: SAT) and reducing from it to others is the standard strategy."""),
           ("3-SAT ≤ₚ Independent Set (the reduction pattern)",
            """Given a formula with m clauses of three literals, build a graph with a triangle for every clause (one vertex per literal) and connect every literal to its negation in other clauses. **Claim:** the formula is satisfiable iff the graph has an independent set of size m. (⇒) Take a satisfying assignment and choose one true literal in every clause; no two chosen vertices are adjacent (clause triangles contain only one chosen, and contradictory literals cannot both be true). (⇐) An independent set of size m has exactly one vertex in each triangle; set those literals true — consistent because no literal and its negation are both chosen. The graph has 3m vertices, so the reduction is polynomial. Since Independent Set ≡ Clique (complement graph) ≡ Vertex Cover (complement of the set), these are NP-complete as well."""),
       ],
       notes=[
           ("The landscape of classes",
            """P ⊆ NP ⊆ PSPACE ⊆ EXPTIME, and P ≠ EXPTIME is known; whether P = NP is open. NP is the class of problems with polynomial-time **verifiable** certificates (a Hamiltonian cycle, a satisfying assignment, a subset with the target sum). NP-hard problems are at least as hard as everything in NP and may lie outside NP (the halting problem is NP-hard); NP-complete = NP ∩ NP-hard. Known NP-complete problems: SAT, 3-SAT (but 2-SAT is in P), clique, vertex cover, independent set, Hamiltonian cycle, TSP (decision version), subset-sum, 3-colouring, partition, bin packing (decision), integer linear programming. In P: shortest path, MST, matching, linear programming, primality (AKS), 2-colourability, Eulerian circuit."""),
           ("Common GATE conclusions",
            """If A ≤ₚ B and B ∈ P then A ∈ P; if A ≤ₚ B and A is NP-hard then B is NP-hard; if B ∈ NP and A ≤ₚ B then A ∈ NP. A problem in P is in NP and in co-NP. Showing a problem is in NP means giving a polynomial verifier; showing it is NP-complete additionally needs a polynomial reduction **from** a known NP-complete problem — reducing the other way proves nothing. The decision and optimisation versions of a problem are polynomially equivalent in difficulty for most natural problems."""),
       ])
